{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>וַיְהִי קוֹלוֹת וּבְרָקִים.</b> Some interpreters of the Torah wanted to give a reason for what these קוֹלוֹת and קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר were, and each one makes allusions by way of derash according to his own understanding. And what seems right to me is to add a comparison in this matter, since we see that the קוֹלוֹת וּבְרָקִים are the opposite of קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר, for the קוֹלוֹת of thunder issuing from within the cloud is a sound that is cut off swiftly, and the lightning flashes forth its arrow, seen one instant and swallowed up the next, but קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר kept going and grew stronger. Therefore it is fair to say that this alludes to two classes of those who receive the Torah — the elders among the disciples of the sages and the elders of the people of the land — for these add wisdom while those become ever more foolish. So too this shofar, which was from the ram of Isaac and traces back to the righteous one — its sound kept going and grew stronger, for the elders among the disciples of the sages always add wisdom. But the coarse, thick matter that traces back to the elders of the people of the land — men of material — their sound and the spark of their light is but for a moment. And the word ״שׁוֹפָר״ they interpreted (Leviticus Rabbah 29:6) from the expression ״שַׁפְּרוּ מַעֲשֵׂיכֶם״, and this accords with our words, and it also alludes to the merit of Isaac, which goes on and grows stronger and whose voice is heard in every generation, going on and growing stronger." "And it further alludes to what they said in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer (31): nothing of the ram of Isaac was for naught — its left horn is the one that was sounded at Mount Sinai, its right horn will be sounded in the future, as it says (Isaiah 27:13) ״וְהָיָה בַיּוֹם הַהוּא יִתָּקַע בְּשׁוֹפָר גָּדוֹל״. And the reason for its greatness: in this world the voice of the Torah is not heard in our land except through promises of the reward of this world, such as wealth and honor that are at its left, and the spiritual reward the Torah did not wish to spell out to those lacking apprehension, for as long as a person is entangled in matter he has no capacity to grasp the nature of spiritual reward. But in the future the earth shall be filled with knowledge, and then HaShem will make the spiritual reward heard for those redeemed by HaShem — and length of days at its right — in a world that is altogether long; and this is an added sound, stronger than the sound of the giving of the Torah. Therefore it says ״וַיְהִי קוֹל הַשּׁוֹפָר הוֹלֵךְ וְחָזֵק מְאֹד״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:28.998095353Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}